import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  role="visitor"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: "Home", href: "/" },
    { label: "Methods & quality" },
    { label: "Limitations" },
  ]}
  backHref="/methods/evaluation-framework"
>
  <div class="mx-auto px-4 py-8 max-w-4xl prose prose-gray">
    <h1>Limitations</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      The SUM platform provides evidence-based insights and decision support, but results must
      be interpreted with awareness of the following limitations.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Statistical limitations — association vs causation</h2>
      <p>
        The impact analysis model identifies <strong>statistical associations</strong> between
        mobility measures and KPI changes — not causal relationships. A measure that correlates
        with positive KPI changes in several cities may not be the direct cause of those changes.
        Other factors (local policy context, infrastructure, demographics) may be responsible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ridge regression cannot establish causation. Results indicate which measures tend to
        co-occur with positive outcomes, which informs — but should not replace — local expert
        judgment and feasibility assessment.
      </p>

      <h3>Sample size</h3>
      <p>
        The platform currently covers a limited number of cities. Statistical estimates become
        more reliable as more cities contribute data. Evidence strength badges (Limited / Moderate
        / Strong) communicate how much data coverage underlies each result. Results with
        "Limited" evidence should be treated with caution.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Data coverage</h2>
      <p>
        Not all cities have collected data for all KPIs. Coverage varies by:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>KPI type</strong> — some KPIs require resources (surveys, sensors) not all cities have</li>
        <li><strong>Time period</strong> — not all cities have both baseline and post-implementation measurements</li>
        <li><strong>Missing data</strong> — some cities have partial before/after data, limiting comparative analysis</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Analyses that aggregate across cities are based on the subset of cities with available data.
        This means results may reflect the specific characteristics of that subset rather than all
        European cities in general.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Interpretation caveats</h2>
      <p>
        When using the platform's results:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Context matters</strong> — A measure that ranked highly in one set of cities may
          not be appropriate for your city's specific transport mix, population, or regulatory context.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>MCDA rankings reflect stated priorities</strong> — The PROMETHEE rankings are based
          on goal weights from expert surveys. Different stakeholder weightings can yield different
          rankings. All three perspectives (Regulatory Authorities, Public Transport Operators, NSM
          Providers) should be considered.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Data freshness</strong> — Check the validation date for each city's data. Older
          data may not reflect current conditions in that city.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Use alongside local expertise</strong> — Platform results are decision support,
          not prescriptions. Local feasibility studies, stakeholder engagement, and regulatory
          requirements remain essential inputs to any mobility planning decision.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <nav class="not-prose mt-8 flex gap-4 text-sm">
      <a href="/methods/glossary" class="text-primary hover:underline">Next: Glossary →</a>
      <a href="/methods/models" class="text-primary hover:underline">← How the models work</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</Layout>
